ef 1075 #edb/ref 17592186447045 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186447051 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186447047 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186447051 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186447049 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186447051 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186447051 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186447051 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186447045 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186447051 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "1cfb4b04-4369-55b5-b86c-1bcf8c68a19d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186447052 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186447035 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186447052 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186447037 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186447052 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186447039 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186447052 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186447040 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186447052 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186447046 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186447052 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186447052 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186447052 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186447037 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186447052 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "4e2c51c7-77b4-56a2-ab4a-cf386667088e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186447053 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186447032 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186447053 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186447034 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186447053 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186447041 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186447053 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186447043 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186447053 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186447050 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186447053 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186447053 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186447053 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186447050 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186447053 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "55e1e424-505b-5097-b514-ba9bc2cc90d9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186447054 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186447031 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186447054 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186447033 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186447054 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186447036 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186447054 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186447038 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186447054 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186447048 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186447054 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186447054 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186447054 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186447031 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186447054 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "f2baa79b-54b3-5737-acdd-b3964add504b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186447055 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186447054 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186447055 #edb/ref 1069 "q-107537" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186447055 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186447055 #edb/ref 1071 "Let $B=\\{{b}_{1},{b}_{2}\\}$ and $C=\\{{c}_{1},{c}_{2},{c}_{3}\\}$ be bases of ${R}^{2}$ and ${R}^{3},$ respectively. \n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $\\{{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\}$ is a basis of ${R}^{2}$\n\nII. $\\{{b}_{1},2{b}_{2}\\}$ is a basis of ${R}^{2}$\n\nIII. $\\{{c}_{1},{c}_{2},{c}_{1}+{c}_{2}\\}$ is a basis of ${R}^{3}$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186447055 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "2dd6c5c5-53ed-575f-8c9f-dc822f51b9e6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186447055 #edb/ref 1074 "Since $B$ and $C$ are bases, the following properties must hold:\n\n- $B$ and $C$ are maximally linearly independent sets in ${R}^{2}$ and ${R}^{3},$ respectively, and\n\n- $B$ spans ${R}^{2},$ while $C$ spans ${R}^{3}.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is false. The set $\\{{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\}$ is not a basis of ${R}^{2}$ because the set contains vectors from another vector space.\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, the equation\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}{b}_{1}+{x}_{2}(2{b}_{2}) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nis equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}{b}_{1}+(2{x}_{2}){b}_{2} & =0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich, since $\\{{b}_{1},{b}_{2}\\}$ is linearly independent, gives\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}=0 \\\\ 2{x}_{2}=0\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}=0 \\\\ {x}_{2}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAlso, $\\{{b}_{1},2{b}_{2}\\}$ spans $V$ since any linear combination of vectors from $B$ can be expressed as a linear combination of $\\{{b}_{1},2{b}_{2}\\}:$\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{b}_{1}+{x}_{2}{b}_{2}={x}_{1}{b}_{1}+\\frac{{x}_{2}}{2}{b}_{2}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. The set $\\{{c}_{1},{c}_{2},{c}_{1}+{c}_{2}\\}$ is not a basis of ${R}^{3}$ because the third vector is a linear combination of ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}.$ This means that the set is linearly dependent and therefore, cannot be a basis.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only\"." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186447056 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186447053 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186447056 #edb/ref 1069 "q-107130" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186447056 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186447056 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the vector space ${R}_{2}[t]$ of all polynomials in the variable $t$ over $R$ with degrees at most $2.$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $\\{2,{t}^{2}\\}$ is a linearly independent set in ${R}_{2}[t]$\n\nII. $2+{t}^{2}$ can be expressed as a linear combination of $\\{2,{t}^{2}\\}$\n\nIII. $\\{2,{t}^{2}\\}$ is a basis of ${R}_{2}[t]$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186447056 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "a024553e-c27e-5c68-a828-a6ec8223e683" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186447056 #edb/ref 1074 "Let's examine each of the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, the equation \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}\\cdot 2+{x}_{2}\\cdot {t}^{2}=0,\n$$\n\n \nis true if and only if ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}=0.$ So, the set is linearly independent.\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed,\n\n$$\n2+{t}^{2}=1\\cdot 2+1\\cdot {t}^{2}.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. For example, the polynomial $t$ can't be written as a linear combination of\n\n$$\n\\{2,{t}^{2}\\}\n$$\n\nsince all these combinations result in polynomials without terms of the form $at.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186447057 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186447051 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186447057 #edb/ref 1069 "q-107374" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186447057 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186447057 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the vector space ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$ of all $2\\times 2$ matrices over $R.$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $\\{[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\}$ is a linearly independent set in ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$\n\nII. $[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ 4 & 0\\end{aligned}]$ can be expressed as a linear combination of $\\{[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\}$\n\nIII. $\\{[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\}$ is a basis of ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186447057 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "ac0d302b-9680-52bd-b447-e8dfc12a3304" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186447057 #edb/ref 1074 "Let's examine each of the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Notice that\n\n$$\n1\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]+0\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]+0\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]+0\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nwhere the first coefficient is nonzero, so the set is linearly dependent. In general, any set that contains a zero-matrix is linearly dependent.\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed,\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ 4 & 0\\end{aligned}]=0\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]+1\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]+(-2)\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]+4\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since the set is not linearly independent, it can't form a basis.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186447058 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186447052 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186447058 #edb/ref 1069 "q-107941" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186447058 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186447058 #edb/ref 1071 "Let $B=\\{{b}_{1},{b}_{2},{b}_{3}\\}$ be a basis of a vector space $V.$ \n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $\\{{b}_{1},{b}_{1}+{b}_{2}\\}$ is linearly dependent\n\nII. $\\{{b}_{1},{b}_{2},2{b}_{3}\\}$ is a basis for $V$\n\nIII. $\\{{b}_{1},{b}_{3},{b}_{1}+{b}_{3}\\}$ is linearly dependent" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186447058 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "c626f6f5-d2a8-58e2-a687-8abd5e7a0913" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186447058 #edb/ref 1074 "Since $B$ is a basis, the following properties must hold:\n\n- $B$ is a maximally linearly independent set of vectors in $V,$ and\n\n- $B$ spans $V.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is false. Since if $\\{{b}_{1},{b}_{1}+{b}_{2}\\}$ is linearly dependent then there exist ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ such that \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{b}_{1}+{x}_{2}({b}_{1}+{b}_{2})=0,\n$$\n\nwhere at least one of $x$'s is not zero. But then \n\n$$\n({x}_{1}+{x}_{2}){b}_{1}+{x}_{2}{b}_{2}+0{b}_{3}=0,\n$$\n\nwhich would mean that $B$ is linearly dependent. This would contradict the fact that $B$ is a basis.\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, the equation\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}{b}_{1}+{x}_{2}{b}_{2}+{x}_{3}(2{b}_{3}) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nis equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}{b}_{1}+{x}_{2}{b}_{2}+(2{x}_{3}){b}_{3} & =0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich, since $\\{{b}_{1},{b}_{2},{b}_{3}\\}$ is linearly independent, gives\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}=0 \\\\ {x}_{2}=0 \\\\ 2{x}_{3}=0\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}=0 \\\\ {x}_{2}=0 \\\\ {x}_{3}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAlso, $\\{{b}_{1},{b}_{2},2{b}_{3}\\}$ spans $V$ since any linear combination of vectors from $B$ can be expressed as a linear combination of $\\{{b}_{1},{b}_{2},2{b}_{3}\\}:$\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{b}_{1}+{x}_{2}{b}_{2}+{x}_{3}{b}_{3}={x}_{1}{b}_{1}+{x}_{2}{b}_{2}+\\frac{{x}_{3}}{2}(2{b}_{3})\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, the third vector is a linear combination of ${b}_{1}$ and ${b}_{3}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
